 have a  series of  of they'll be in bill form soon but but  ideas we're talking about  get rid of 230 private right of action  like big tech  makes a moderate content moderation  decision about you they got to tell you  why  there needs to be some training why they  do that and frankly if they do it and  like desantis did in florida if you're a  candidate for office and they do those  kind of things  there should be a right of action you  have to hold them accountable so that's  something we're looking at  and then frankly i think you know  justice thomas has i think more than  hinted  that he wants to address this issue um  and  talking about dick big tech and the  power they have so what we're also  looking at doing is speeding up  the antitrust uh actions in our court  system so it gets to the supreme court  and we can get an answer after all the  trump administration  both the ftc brought uh any trust action  against facebook and the justice  department  has brought in any trust action against  google and we'll see how that all shakes  out with the biden  doj but i think that's where it has to  focus versus what  the democrats are talking about doing  which is just giving more power to  the ftc which i think would translate  into  um big government colluding with big  tech  to further uh stick it to conservatives  right which is pretty much what's  happening already on on the 230 point  uh my audience is all they all know  about the difference between platform  and publisher and all that  the pushback on getting rid of 230 which  i'm basically for getting rid of it so i  think  we're pretty much lined up there the  pushback though is that if you get rid  of the protections  that basically all of these platforms  twitter youtube facebook etc that they  will have to censor more  because they'll be worried about the  liability what would you say to that  legitimate concern there's also the  concern that you know they're so big  they can handle the  the the the the lawsuits that come when  they do censor people  and versus the competition that we want  to have happen  but i just think the fundamental issue  is look if they're  if they're making publishing decisions  editorial decisions  they're not supposed to have the  liability protection and that's we know  that  that's what they're doing so i i think  you just have to look at sort of the  fundamental question say okay you're  making those editorial decisions you  don't get the protection  and then and then then then go from  there but that's why i think you also  need the other things i talked about  um to actually address this again what  what concerns me most is  in an effort to deal with censorship we  have we have people embracing this idea  that oh we got to give the government  the the bureaucracy the agencies more  regulatory power  and i just think that's a mess and  frankly we just saw this we just saw  this with jin saki the spokesperson  for the president of the united states i  mean think about what she said  she said big tech are now where so many  people get their news i.e  they're the press and so she said we are  working with the press  to stop certain information from getting  to we the people the american people  that that is a direct violation of their  first amendment freedom of the press  the liberties we have that's scary and  they got some list of 12 people who they  said are putting out misinformation so  there's that sort of fundamental  question i think that's what's what the  concern is is  that marriage of big government and big  tech  i think only means conservatives get it  worse  when you hear saki say something like  that her actual  the word that to me was the where the  real bell went off was she said  we flag content for facebook  the implication is we're doing it  together right we're flagging it  for them when you hear that do you  ever go to your democratic counterparts  and go guys  look we may not agree on tax rates we  may not agree on abortion or blah blah  blah  but i thought we at least agreed on  first amendment stuff do they do they  take that meeting do they take that call  they used to i i don't know if they  would now and frankly my i have my i'm  skeptical they would now because  it has gotten so so partisan and we've  had a number of democrats some of them  on on the judiciary committee the  committee that's supposed to safeguard  the bill of rights the constitution and  americans fundamental rights and  liberties  we we've had so many of them cheer on  big tech  when they sense are conservative so it's  not the same  it used to be that you could work with i  mean i always make this  this uh tell this story dennis kucinich  former member is a friend of mine i mean  i talked to him on the phone a few  months i just texted him yesterday  because i saw him on a clip on tucker uh  dennis is an old-school liberal who says  let's let's make the best arguments to  have the debate and he was with us on  civil liberties  but that's not where today's left is  today's left is authoritarian and you're  seeing it from the spokesperson  of the president of the united states  estates in the white house  uh briefing room so it's a different  animal today  and again i think that's why you're so  concerned and i'm so concerned so many  americans are  about what's really happening to our  freedoms yeah you know dennis kucinich  is actually a perfect example when he  ran for president which was you know  probably 16 years ago something like  that yeah he was the furthest  left democrat at that point and  basically the only people who will talk  to him now  are tucker and fox news they would never  put on cnn  and he was a champion of civil we we  were on the oversight committee together  when democrats had uh power back when  demo uh when when when dennis was in  congress  he chaired this subcommittee i was the  ranking man and when it flipped i was  chairman he was ranking member  and there were issues where we could  work together typically they were civil  like we were both concerned about facial  recognition technology and how  law enforcement might use that to  violate our so we could work together  and when we didn't agree he was a true  liberal like let's have the debate let's  have get your best hold make your best  argument  let's see who wins after it's over we'll  shake hands and we'll get ready for the  next issue and have the debate  that is so different from what we see  today where it's it's so  the the vitriol and going after people  and the canceling and everything else it  is a completely different animal  i i long for the uh for the people like  kucinich like dennis kucinich who's a  good man  and and believes in so many the same  things we do when it comes to civil  liberties  different on policy where we're miles  apart on other policy issues  but um you know we need more of that  frankly in the united states congress  i think i've asked this question to  every congressman or senator that i've  had on and i'd love to know your take  too do you know  when do you have a moment or was there a  person  that you can point to when the democrats  really went off the deep end with this  stuff when it was  okay no more talking no more negotiating  you guys are all  bad guys nazis whatever nonsense they're  throwing at you do you remember when it  happened  well i mean i would probably point to  november 8 2016 when donald trump got  elected president because we know what  president trump like an individual by  the way who i think was  did an amazing job as president did more  what he said than any  any president certainly in my lifetime  uh accomplished so much  with relentless uh attacks from the left  and the  and the democrats and the media and  frankly too many republicans and still  got so much done  um but i think it probably started then  now there may be something else but the  first thing that popped to mind was  the day he wins election demo